RP2040 MACRO PAD — CIRCUITPYTHON STARTER PACK
============================================

WHAT'S INCLUDED
- code.py: CircuitPython firmware (renamed from main.py because CircuitPython runs code.py)
- boot.py: enables the second USB CDC data serial interface
- windows_companion.py: simple Windows app to choose mode and send media metadata
- requirements.txt: Python packages for the Windows app

IMPORTANT HARDWARE NOTE
The GPIO numbers in code.py are PLACEHOLDERS. Earlier project discussion established:
- RP2040 / Raspberry Pi Pico-style board with USB-C
- 1.9-inch 8-pin ST7789 display
- EC11 rotary encoder with push switch
- four hot-swap key sockets
- addressable RGB LEDs (exact type/count/voltage not yet verified)
But the exact KiCad pin assignments were not confirmed. Edit the PIN CONFIGURATION
section in code.py to match your actual schematic before connecting/powering the board.
The display's width, height, rotation, rowstart/colstart and CS wiring may need adjustment.

INSTALL ON THE PICO
1. Install CircuitPython for your exact RP2040 board from circuitpython.org.
2. Copy the CircuitPython library files/folders from the matching Adafruit CircuitPython
   library bundle onto CIRCUITPY/lib:
   - adafruit_st7789.mpy
   - adafruit_display_text/ (folder)
   - adafruit_hid/ (folder; often already included in the bundle)
   - neopixel.mpy if using addressable LEDs
3. Copy boot.py and code.py to the CIRCUITPY root.
4. If your CircuitPython version already exposes USB CDC data, boot.py is still safe to use.
5. Optional custom background: copy background.bmp to CIRCUITPY root. Make it exactly the
   display's logical resolution after rotation (commonly 320x170 in landscape or 170x320
   in portrait). The firmware loads it automatically; if absent, it uses a solid background.

WINDOWS COMPANION
1. Install Python 3.10+.
2. Open Command Prompt in this folder and run:
   py -m pip install -r requirements.txt
3. Run:
   py windows_companion.py
4. Select the Pico's USB CDC data COM port and click Connect.
5. Choose AUTO / SPOTIFY / YOUTUBE / OSU / CUSTOM.
Windows media metadata depends on the app/browser publishing a Windows media session.

DEFAULT BUTTON MAPPING (edit do_key() in code.py as desired)
- K1: Spotify previous track; otherwise Left Arrow (YouTube seeks back 5 seconds)
- K2: media Play/Pause; also sends K in browser mode
- K3: Spotify next track; otherwise Right Arrow (YouTube seeks forward 5 seconds)
- K4: Spotify next track; YouTube next video (Shift+N).

IMPORTANT LIMITATIONS / NEXT STEPS
- This is a configurable starter, not yet verified against your physical PCB.
- USB media keys work independently of the Windows companion app, but the YouTube keyboard
  shortcuts act on whichever app/window currently has keyboard focus. Click the video first.
- YouTube's native J/L shortcuts seek 10 seconds; Left/Right Arrow seek 5 seconds when the
  player is focused. K toggles play/pause.
- The Windows companion currently sends media title/artist/app metadata. It does not yet
  extract osu! beatmap difficulty or star rating. Those require a dedicated osu! data/log
  integration, which must be added and tested against your installed osu! version.
- Custom background support loads a BMP at the exact logical display resolution; it does not
  resize/crop images. Confirm display rotation and dimensions first.
- No app can guarantee YouTube metadata unless the browser exposes its media session to Windows.

SAFETY
Do not power LEDs from a GPIO pin. Confirm LED voltage, common ground and data-level
requirements against the exact LED strip/module. Verify all pin assignments before use.
